      <p class="hero-sub">ADA website compliance means your site meets WCAG 2.2 Level AA. People with disabilities can then see, hear, navigate and use all of your content. Title III of the ADA expects this from any business open to the public.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a href="#faq" class="btn btn-whatsapp btn-lg" id="hero-p3-faq">Jump to FAQ</a>
        <a href="#cta" class="btn btn-outline-light btn-lg">Get a Free Audit Quote</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="page-layout">
        <article class="prose-section">

          <div class="tldr-box" role="note" aria-label="Key takeaways">
            <div class="tldr-label"><Icon name="zap" class="lucide-icon" aria-hidden="true" size={20} strokeWidth={2} />TL;DR — What Is ADA Compliance?</div>
            <ul>
              <li>ADA website compliance means your site meets WCAG 2.2 Level AA accessibility standards.</li>
              <li>Required under Title III of the ADA for any business open to the public — no size exemption.</li>
              <li>Over 4,000 federal ADA lawsuits were filed in 2024; average settlement is $12,000–$75,000.</li>
              <li>WCAG 2.2 Level AA is the current best-practice standard, building on WCAG 2.1 with 9 new criteria.</li>
              <li>Automated tools detect only 30–40% of violations; professional manual audits are required.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <h2>What Is ADA Compliance for Websites?</h2>
          <p><strong>ADA website compliance</strong> means your website conforms to the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 Level AA. Title III of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) is the law behind it.</p>
          <p>ADA compliant websites work for people with visual, hearing, motor, cognitive and neurological disabilities. That includes people who use screen readers, a keyboard only, voice control or other assistive technology.</p>
          <p>The ADA became law in 1990. Federal courts have since applied it to websites. Businesses open to the public, called "places of public accommodation," must offer accessible services — and that includes their website.</p>
          <p>In April 2024 the Department of Justice (DOJ) set WCAG 2.1 Level AA as the standard for state and local government websites. Courts and plaintiffs now use the same benchmark for private businesses.</p>
          <div class="definition-box">
            <strong>One-sentence definition:</strong> ADA website compliance means your site is built so that every user — regardless of disability — can access the same information and complete the same tasks as any other user.
          </div>

          <h2>ADA vs. WCAG: What's the Difference?</h2>
          <div class="table-wrapper">
            <table class="prose-table" aria-label="ADA versus WCAG comparison">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col"></th>
                  <th scope="col">ADA</th>
                  <th scope="col">WCAG</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>What it is</strong></td><td>U.S. civil rights law</td><td>Technical accessibility standard</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Who created it</strong></td><td>U.S. Congress (1990)</td><td>W3C (World Wide Web Consortium)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>What it requires</strong></td><td>Accessible websites for the public</td><td>Specific technical criteria (1.1.1, 1.4.3, etc.)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Current version</strong></td><td>ADA Title III (ongoing)</td><td>WCAG 2.2 (September 2023)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Enforcement</strong></td><td>Private lawsuits + DOJ</td><td>Guidelines only (courts apply to measure ADA)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Level required</strong></td><td>Not specified in law</td><td>Courts use WCAG 2.1 Level AA as minimum</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p><strong>Plain-language summary:</strong> ADA is the law; WCAG is the rulebook courts use to determine whether you've followed the law. Meeting WCAG 2.2 Level AA is the most defensible standard for WCAG ADA compliance today.</p>

          <h2>The POUR Principles: How WCAG Defines Accessibility</h2>
          <p>WCAG 2.2 organizes all accessibility requirements under four principles, known as POUR:</p>
          <div class="pour-grid">
            {pourCards.map((card) => (
              <div class="pour-card">
                <div class="pour-letter" aria-hidden="true">{card.letter}</div>
                <h3>{card.letter} — {card.title}</h3>
                <p>{card.desc}</p>
                <ul>
                  {card.examples.map((ex) => <li>{ex}</li>)}
                </ul>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>

          <h2>WCAG ADA Compliance: Which Version and Level Do You Need?</h2>
          <p>WCAG has three levels: A, AA and AAA. Level A is the bare minimum. Level AAA is not practical for most sites. <strong>Level AA is the target for WCAG ADA compliance</strong> in lawsuits, settlements and the DOJ rule.</p>
          <p>For the version, WCAG 2.1 AA is the legal floor most courts cite. WCAG 2.2 AA adds nine success criteria and includes everything in 2.1. Building to 2.2 AA covers both.</p>

          <h2>What Makes a Website ADA Compliant? A Practical Checklist</h2>
          <p>ADA compliant websites share the same core features. Use this list as a first check of your own site:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Text alternatives</strong> — every meaningful image has alt text; decorative images are hidden from screen readers.</li>
            <li><strong>Keyboard access</strong> — menus, forms, pop-ups and checkout all work with the Tab and Enter keys.</li>
            <li><strong>Visible focus</strong> — users can always see which element is selected.</li>
            <li><strong>Color contrast</strong> — body text reaches at least 4.5:1 against its background.</li>
            <li><strong>Form labels and errors</strong> — every field has a label; errors say what went wrong and how to fix it.</li>
            <li><strong>Headings and landmarks</strong> — one H1, a logical H2/H3 outline, and clear page regions.</li>
            <li><strong>Media</strong> — videos have captions; audio has a transcript.</li>
            <li><strong>Documents</strong> — PDFs are tagged and readable by screen readers.</li>
            <li><strong>Resizing</strong> — content still works at 200% zoom and on small screens.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Automated tools catch only part of this list. Keyboard, focus and screen reader issues need a manual test. Our <a href="/ada-compliance-audit-small-business">ADA audit for small business</a> covers every item.</p>

          <h2>Software ADA Compliance: Web Apps, Portals and SaaS</h2>
          <p>Software ADA compliance follows the same rule: WCAG 2.2 AA. It applies to customer portals, booking systems, SaaS dashboards and mobile web apps your customers use.</p>
          <p>Software adds extra risk points. Watch for:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Custom controls (date pickers, sliders, drag-and-drop) without keyboard support or ARIA roles</li>
            <li>Dynamic updates and alerts that screen readers never announce</li>
            <li>Session timeouts with no warning or way to extend</li>
            <li>Logins that rely on puzzles or memory tests (WCAG 2.2 criterion 3.3.8)</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Public sector and enterprise buyers often also ask for a VPAT. That document reports how your software meets WCAG and Section 508.</p>

          <h2>How to Make Your Website ADA Compliant in 5 Steps</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Run a free scan</strong> with WAVE, axe or Lighthouse to find obvious errors.</li>
            <li><strong>Commission a manual audit</strong> against WCAG 2.2 AA to find the 60–70% that tools miss.</li>
            <li><strong>Fix the code</strong> — start with checkout, forms, navigation and your most visited pages.</li>
            <li><strong>Re-test</strong> to confirm each fix works with a keyboard and a screen reader.</li>
            <li><strong>Publish an accessibility statement</strong> and re-check after every major site update.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>Small businesses can claim up to $5,000 a year back through the <a href="/disabled-access-credit-tax">ADA tax credit (Form 8826)</a>. Already received a legal notice? Read our <a href="/received-ada-demand-letter">ADA demand letter response guide</a>.</p>

          <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <p>This FAQ is optimized to answer the most common questions AI systems and search engines have about ADA compliance for websites. Each answer is designed for direct citation.</p>
          <FaqAccordion items={faqItems} />

        </article>

